Safer ways to cross-reference with your vault:

## Option 1: Compare locally without printing values

If you have the vault values exported into another env file, you can compare names and values locally with a script that only prints match status.

Example `compare-env.js`:

```js
const fs = require('fs');

function parseEnv(path) {
  const content = fs.readFileSync(path, 'utf8');
  const result = {};

  for (const line of content.split('\n')) {
    const trimmed = line.trim();
    if (!trimmed || trimmed.startsWith('#')) continue;

    const index = trimmed.indexOf('=');
    if (index === -1) continue;

    const key = trimmed.slice(0, index);
    const value = trimmed.slice(index + 1);
    result[key] = value;
  }

  return result;
}

const repoEnv = parseEnv('.env.test');
const vaultEnv = parseEnv('.env.vault');

for (const key of Object.keys(repoEnv)) {
  if (!(key in vaultEnv)) {
    console.log(`${key}: missing from vault export`);
  } else if (repoEnv[key] === vaultEnv[key]) {
    console.log(`${key}: MATCH`);
  } else {
    console.log(`${key}: different`);
  }
}
```

Run:

```bash
node compare-env.js
```

This avoids printing the credentials themselves.

## Option 2: Use fingerprints

You can generate SHA-256 fingerprints locally and compare those with vault-side fingerprints:

```bash
awk -F= '/^[A-Z0-9_]+=/{print $1"="$(substr($0, index($0,$2)))}' .env.test \
  | while IFS='=' read -r key value; do
      printf "%s: " "$key"
      printf "%s" "$value" | sha256sum | cut -d' ' -f1
    done
```

This prints hashes, not raw secret prefixes. Treat hashes as sensitive metadata if the values may be low entropy.

